The problem asks for  

\[
P(\text{all four match}\mid\text{at least two match})=\frac{P(\text{all four match})}{P(\text{at least two match})}.
\]

---

### 1. Model the overlap

Jen’s 4 chosen numbers are a fixed set of size \(K=4\) from a population of \(N=10\).  
The random draw also selects \(n=4\) numbers without replacement.  

The number of matches \(X\) follows a hypergeometric distribution:

\[
P(X=k)=\frac{\binom{K}{k}\binom{N-K}{n-k}}{\binom{N}{n}},\qquad k=0,1,2,3,4.
\]

Here \(N-K=6\).

---

### 2. Compute \(P(X=4)\) (grand prize)

\[
P(X=4)=\frac{\binom{4}{4}\binom{6}{0}}{\binom{10}{4}}=\frac{1\cdot1}{210}=\frac1{210}.
\]

---

### 3. Compute \(P(X\ge 2)\)

\[
\begin{aligned}
P(X=2)&=\frac{\binom{4}{2}\binom{6}{2}}{\binom{10}{4}}
      =\frac{6\cdot15}{210}=\frac{90}{210}=\frac{3}{7},\\[4pt]
P(X=3)&=\frac{\binom{4}{3}\binom{6}{1}}{\binom{10}{4}}
      =\frac{4\cdot6}{210}=\frac{24}{210}=\frac{4}{35},\\[4pt]
P(X=4)&=\frac1{210}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Summing:

\[
P(X\ge2)=\frac{90+24+1}{210}=\frac{115}{210}=\frac{23}{42}.
\]

---

### 4. Conditional probability

\[
P(\text{all four}\mid\text{at least two})=
\frac{P(X=4)}{P(X\ge2)}=
\frac{\frac1{210}}{\frac{23}{42}}=
\frac{1}{210}\cdot\frac{42}{23}
=\frac{42}{210\cdot23}
=\frac{1}{5}\cdot\frac{1}{23}
=\frac{1}{115}.
\]

Thus \(m=1,\; n=115\) (already relatively prime).  

\[
m+n=1+115=116.
\]

\[
\boxed{116}
\]

