Thus the rhombus is centrally symmetric about origin. For any rhombus, the diagonals intersect at the center (midpoint of both diagonals). So if the intersection is at origin, then the rhombus is centered at origin. So the vertices are symmetric: if A is a vertex, then C is opposite vertex such that O is midpoint of AC. Similarly B and D are opposite vertices.

