We have counts of exactly two owners: includes those who own exactly two of the four items. Since C is always owned, exactly two owners must own C plus exactly one of D, G, S. Because they own C always, so exactly two means they own C and exactly one of the other three. Exactly three owners must own C plus exactly two of D, G, S. Exactly four owners own all four.

Thus we can compute counts of exactly one of D, G, S (i.e., those who own C and exactly one of D, G, S) = a_2 = 437.

Similarly, exactly two of D, G, S (plus C) = a_3 = 234.

Exactly three of D, G, S (plus C) = a_4 = x.

Thus we can also compute the total number of owners of D, G, S individually.

Let’s denote:

Let n1 = number of residents who own exactly one of D, G, S (i.e., own C + exactly one of D,G,S). That's a_2 = 437.

Let n2 = number of residents who own exactly two of D,G,S (plus C). That's a_3 = 234.

Let n3 = number of residents who own exactly three of D,G,S (plus C). That's a_4 = x.

Let n0 = number of residents who own none of D,G,S (only C). That's a_1 = 156.

Now we can compute total counts of D, G, S:

Each resident who owns exactly one of D,G,S contributes 1 to the sum of sizes of D+G+S. Those who own exactly two contribute 2, those who own exactly three contribute 3, those who own none contribute 0.

Thus sum_{sets} |D|+|G|+|S| = n1*1 + n2*2 + n3*3 = 437 + 2*234 + 3*x = 437 + 468 + 3x = 905 + 3x.

But we know |D|+|G|+|S| = 195+367+562 = 1124.

Thus 905 + 3x = 1124 => 3x = 1124 - 905 = 219 => x = 73. Same result.

Thus answer is 73.

