The family of unit‑length segments with endpoints on the axes can be described by the line equation  

\[
\frac{x}{\cos\theta}+\frac{y}{\sin\theta}=1,\qquad 0<\theta<\frac{\pi}{2},
\]

where the intercepts are \((\cos\theta,0)\) and \((0,\sin\theta)\).  
A point \((x,y)\) lies on some segment iff there exists \(\theta\) satisfying the above.  
The envelope of this family is found by also setting \(\partial/\partial\theta=0\), giving  

\[
x\sin^3\theta = y\cos^3\theta \;\Longrightarrow\; y = x\tan^3\theta .
\]

Substituting into the line equation yields the parametric envelope  

\[
x(t)=\frac{1}{(1+t^2)^{3/2}},\qquad 
y(t)=\frac{t^3}{(1+t^2)^{3/2}},\qquad t\ge0.
\]

The segment \(\overline{AB}\) has intercepts \(\bigl(\tfrac12,0\bigr)\) and \(\bigl(0,\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\bigr)\), so its line equation is  

\[
\frac{x}{1/2}+\frac{y}{\sqrt3/2}=1\;\Longrightarrow\;2x+\frac{2}{\sqrt3}y=1.
\]

Intersecting this line with the envelope:

\[
2\frac{1}{(1+t^2)^{3/2}}+\frac{2}{\sqrt3}\frac{t^3}{(1+t^2)^{3/2}}=1
\]
\[
\Longrightarrow\;(1+t^2)^{3/2}=2\Bigl(1+\frac{t^3}{\sqrt3}\Bigr).
\]

Testing \(t=\sqrt3\) gives  

\[
(1+3)^{3/2}=4^{3/2}=8,\qquad 
2\Bigl(1+\frac{(\sqrt3)^3}{\sqrt3}\Bigr)=2(1+3)=8,
\]

so \(t=\sqrt3\) satisfies the equation. This is the unique intersection within the segment.

Thus  

\[
C=\Bigl(\frac{1}{8},\frac{3\sqrt3}{8}\Bigr).
\]

Compute \(OC^2\):

\[
OC^2 = \Bigl(\frac18\Bigr)^2+\Bigl(\frac{3\sqrt3}{8}\Bigr)^2
      = \frac1{64}+\frac{9\cdot3}{64}
      = \frac{1+27}{64}
      = \frac{28}{64}
      = \frac{7}{16}.
\]

Hence \(p=7,\;q=16\) (relatively prime) and  

\[
p+q = 7+16 = 23.
\]

\[
\boxed{23}
\]

