\[
L=a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}+\lambda(ab+bc+ca-27)+\mu(abc-23).
\]

Partial derivatives give

\[
\begin{cases}
2a+\lambda(b+c)+\mu bc=0,\\
2b+\lambda(a+c)+\mu ac=0,\\
2c+\lambda(a+b)+\mu ab=0.
\end{cases}
\]

Subtracting pairs yields \((a-b)(2-\lambda-\mu c)=0\), etc.  
Thus at a stationary point either all three sides are equal (impossible here) or two sides are equal.

Let \(a=b=x\) and \(c=y\). Then

\[
x^{2}+2xy=27,\qquad x^{2}y=23.
\]

From the second equation \(y=23/x^{2}\). Substituting into the first:

\[
x^{2}+\frac{46}{x}=27\;\Longrightarrow\;x^{3}-27x+46=0.
\]

Factoring gives

\[
(x-2)(x^{2}+2x-23)=0,
\]

so the positive solutions are  

\[
x=2,\qquad x=-1+2\sqrt6.
\]

