**Solution Overview**

The family \(\mathcal F\) consists of all line segments \(\overline{PQ}\) with  
\(P=(p,0),\; Q=(0,q)\) in the first quadrant and \(\sqrt{p^{2}+q^{2}}=1\).  
Each such segment lies on the line  

\[
\frac{x}{p}+\frac{y}{q}=1\qquad (p,q\ge0,\;p^{2}+q^{2}=1).
\]

The segment \(\overline{AB}\) has endpoints  

\[
A=\Bigl(\frac12,0\Bigr),\qquad B=\Bigl(0,\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\Bigr),
\]

so it corresponds to \(p=\frac12,\;q=\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\) (indeed \((\frac12)^2+(\frac{\sqrt3}{2})^2=1\)).

A point \(C\) on \(\overline{AB}\) belongs to another segment of \(\mathcal F\) iff there exists a different pair \((p,q)\) satisfying the same line equation.  
The set of all lines \(\frac{x}{p}+\frac{y}{q}=1\) with \(p^{2}+q^{2}=1\) has an **envelope**.  
A point on that envelope is intersected by exactly one line of the family; points inside the envelope are intersected by at least two.

Thus the unique point \(C\) on \(\overline{AB}\) that lies on no other segment must be the point where \(\overline{AB}\) is tangent to the envelope.

---

### 1. Find the envelope of the family

Parameterise the family by an angle \(\theta\in[0,\pi/2]\):

\[
p=\cos\theta,\qquad q=\sin\theta.
\]

The line equation becomes  

\[
F(x,y,\theta)=\frac{x}{\cos\theta}+\frac{y}{\sin\theta}-1=0.
\]

The envelope satisfies  

\[
F=0,\qquad \frac{\partial F}{\partial\theta}=0.
\]

Compute the derivative:

\[
\frac{\partial F}{\partial\theta}=x\frac{\sin\theta}{\cos^{2}\theta}
 -y\frac{\cos\theta}{\sin^{2}\theta}=0.
\]

Multiplying by \(\cos^{2}\theta\sin^{2}\theta\) gives  

\[
x\sin^{3}\theta-y\cos^{3}\theta=0
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\tan^{3}\theta=\frac{y}{x}.
\tag{1}
\]

Let \(t=\tan\theta\). Then \(\sin\theta=\frac{t}{\sqrt{1+t^{2}}}\), \(\cos\theta=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1+t^{2}}}\).

Substituting these into \(F=0\):

\[
x\sqrt{1+t^{2}}+\frac{y}{t}\sqrt{1+t^{2}}=1
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\bigl(x+\frac{y}{t}\bigr)\sqrt{1+t^{2}}=1.
\]

Using (1) \(t^{3}=y/x\) gives after simplification  

\[
\bigl(x^{2/3}+y^{2/3}\bigr)^{3/2}=1
\;\Longrightarrow\;
x^{2/3}+y^{2/3}=1.
\]

Hence the envelope is the astroid  

\[
\boxed{x^{2/3}+y^{2/3}=1},\qquad x,y\ge0.
\]

---

### 2. Locate the tangency point of \(\overline{AB}\) with the envelope

The line \(\overline{AB}\) has intercept form  

\[
\frac{x}{\frac12}+\frac{y}{\frac{\sqrt3}{2}}=1
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\frac{2x}{1}+\frac{2y}{\sqrt3}=1
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\frac{x}{\frac12}+\frac{y}{\frac{\sqrt3}{2}}=1.
\]

Parameterise points on \(\overline{AB}\) by a parameter \(s\in[0,1]\):

\[
x=\frac12(1-s),\qquad y=\frac{\sqrt3}{2}s.
\]

Insert into the envelope equation:

\[
\Bigl(\frac12(1-s)\Bigr)^{2/3}
+\Bigl(\frac{\sqrt3}{2}s\Bigr)^{2/3}=1.
\]

Factor out \(2^{-2/3}\):

\[
2^{-2/3}\Bigl[(1-s)^{2/3}+3^{1/3}s^{2/3}\Bigr]=1
\;\Longrightarrow\;
(1-s)^{2/3}+3^{1/3}s^{2/3}=2^{2/3}.
\tag{2}
\]

Define \(f(s)=(1-s)^{2/3}+3^{1/3}s^{2/3}\).  
Differentiating and setting \(f'(s)=0\) yields

\[
-(1-s)^{-1/3}+3^{1/3}s^{-1/3}=0
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\frac{s}{1-s}=3
\;\Longrightarrow\;
s=\frac34.
\]

At \(s=\frac34\), \(f(s)\) attains its maximum, which equals \(2^{2/3}\).  
Thus (2) is satisfied **exactly** at \(s=\frac34\).  
Therefore \(\overline{AB}\) is tangent to the envelope at the point corresponding to \(s=\frac34\).

---

### 3. Coordinates of the tangency point \(C\)

\[
x_C=\frac12\Bigl(1-\frac34\Bigr)=\frac12\cdot\frac14=\frac18,
\qquad
y_C=\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\cdot\frac34=\frac{3\sqrt3}{8}.
\]

So  

\[
C=\Bigl(\frac18,\;\frac{3\sqrt3}{8}\Bigr).
\]

Because this point lies on the envelope, it belongs to **exactly one** segment of \(\mathcal F\), namely \(\overline{AB}\). Hence it is the required unique point.

---

### 4. Compute \(OC^{2}\)

\[
OC^{2}=x_C^{2}+y_C^{2}
=\Bigl(\frac18\Bigr)^{2}
+\Bigl(\frac{3\sqrt3}{8}\Bigr)^{2}
=\frac1{64}+\frac{9\cdot3}{64}
=\frac{1+27}{64}
=\frac{28}{64}
=\frac{7}{16}.
\]

Thus \(p=7,\;q=16\) (already relatively prime) and  

\[
p+q=7+16=23.
\]

---

\[
\boxed{23}
\]

