The family of unit segments \(PQ\) with \(P=(x,0)\) and \(Q=(0,y)\) satisfies  

\[
x^2+y^2=1 .
\]

Each segment lies on the line  

\[
\frac{u}{x}+\frac{v}{y}=1\qquad (u,v)\text{ a point on the segment}.
\]

Writing \(x=\cos\theta,\;y=\sin\theta\ (0\le\theta\le\pi/2)\) gives the line equation  

\[
\sin\theta\,u+\cos\theta\,v=\sin\theta\cos\theta .
\]

The envelope of this family is obtained from  

\[
F(u,v,\theta)=\sin\theta\,u+\cos\theta\,v-\sin\theta\cos\theta=0,
\]
\[
\frac{\partial F}{\partial\theta}=0.
\]

Solving these yields the envelope  

\[
u=\cos^3\theta,\qquad v=\sin^3\theta,
\]
or equivalently  

\[
u^{2/3}+v^{2/3}=1 .
\]

Thus a point lies on **only one** segment of the family precisely when it lies on this envelope.

The segment \(AB\) has endpoints  

\[
A\Bigl(\frac12,0\Bigr),\qquad B\Bigl(0,\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\Bigr)
\]

and equation  

\[
y=\frac{\sqrt3}{2}-\sqrt3\,x .
\]

To find the unique point \(C\) on \(AB\) that is also on the envelope, solve  

\[
x^{2/3}+ \Bigl(\frac{\sqrt3}{2}-\sqrt3\,x\Bigr)^{2/3}=1 .
\]

Parameterising the envelope as \((x,y)=(\cos^3\theta,\sin^3\theta)\) and substituting into the line equation gives  

\[
\sin^3\theta+\sqrt3\cos^3\theta=\frac{\sqrt3}{2}.
\]

Checking \(\theta=\frac{\pi}{3}\) (where \(\cos\theta=\frac12,\ \sin\theta=\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\)) satisfies this equation:

\[
\left(\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)^3+\sqrt3\left(\frac12\right)^3
= \frac{3\sqrt3}{8}+\frac{\sqrt3}{8}
= \frac{4\sqrt3}{8}
= \frac{\sqrt3}{2}.
\]

Hence  

\[
C=\bigl(\cos^3\frac{\pi}{3},\,\sin^3\frac{\pi}{3}\bigr)
= \left(\frac18,\;\frac{3\sqrt3}{8}\right).
\]

Now compute \(OC^2\):

\[
OC^2 = \left(\frac18\right)^2 + \left(\frac{3\sqrt3}{8}\right)^2
= \frac1{64} + \frac{9\cdot3}{64}
= \frac{1+27}{64}
= \frac{28}{64}
= \frac{7}{16}.
\]

Thus \(p=7,\ q=16\) and  

\[
p+q = 7+16 = 23.
\]

\[
\boxed{23}
\]

