**Solution Overview**

The family \(\mathcal F\) consists of all unit‑length segments joining a point \(P=(x,0)\) on the \(x\)-axis to a point \(Q=(0,y)\) on the \(y\)-axis, where  

\[
x^2+y^2=1,\qquad x\ge0,\;y\ge0 .
\]

Each segment lies on the line  

\[
\frac{u}{x}+\frac{v}{y}=1\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad 
\sin\theta\,u+\cos\theta\,v=\sin\theta\cos\theta,
\]

where we set \(x=\cos\theta,\;y=\sin\theta\) (\(0\le\theta\le\frac\pi2\)).  

The envelope of this one‑parameter family of lines gives the boundary of the region covered by all segments. Solving  

\[
F(u,v,\theta)=\sin\theta\,u+\cos\theta\,v-\sin\theta\cos\theta=0,
\]
\[
\frac{\partial F}{\partial\theta}=0
\]

yields the envelope parametrically as  

\[
u=\cos^3\theta,\qquad v=\sin^3\theta .
\]

Hence the envelope satisfies  

\[
u^{2/3}+v^{2/3}=1 .
\]

A point on a segment belonging to \(\mathcal F\) is interior to the region \(\{u^{2/3}+v^{2/3}<1\}\) and is thus shared by many segments.  
A point on the envelope is touched by exactly one segment – the tangent line.  

Therefore the unique point \(C\) on \(\overline{AB}\) that lies on no segment of \(\mathcal F\) other than \(\overline{AB}\) is the intersection of \(\overline{AB}\) with the envelope.

---

### 1. Equation of \(\overline{AB}\)

\(A=\bigl(\tfrac12,0\bigr)\), \(B=\bigl(0,\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\bigr)\).  
The line through \(A\) and \(B\) is

\[
\frac{x}{1/2}+\frac{y}{\sqrt3/2}=1
\;\Longleftrightarrow\;
2x+\frac{2}{\sqrt3}y=1,
\qquad\text{or}\qquad
y=\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\,(1-2x).
\]

---

### 2. Intersection with the envelope

We need \((x,y)\) satisfying both

\[
\begin{cases}
x^{2/3}+y^{2/3}=1,\\[4pt]
2x+\dfrac{2}{\sqrt3}y=1.
\end{cases}
\]

Rather than solving the first equation directly, note that at a tangent point the gradient of \(f(x,y)=x^{2/3}+y^{2/3}\) is normal to \(\overline{AB}\).  

\[
\nabla f =\left(\frac{2}{3}x^{-1/3},\frac{2}{3}y^{-1/3}\right),
\qquad
\text{normal to }\overline{AB} = (2,\;2/\sqrt3).
\]

Thus there exists \(\lambda\) with  

\[
\frac{2}{3}x^{-1/3}=2\lambda,\qquad
\frac{2}{3}y^{-1/3}=\frac{2}{\sqrt3}\lambda.
\]

Dividing gives  

\[
\frac{x^{-1/3}}{y^{-1/3}}=\sqrt3
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\left(\frac{y}{x}\right)^{1/3}=\sqrt3
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\frac{y}{x}= (\sqrt3)^3 =3\sqrt3.
\]

Hence \(y=3\sqrt3\,x\).

Plug this relation into the line equation:

\[
2x+\frac{2}{\sqrt3}(3\sqrt3\,x)=2x+6x=8x=1
\;\Longrightarrow\;
x=\frac18,\qquad
y=3\sqrt3\cdot\frac18=\frac{3\sqrt3}{8}.
\]

Check the envelope condition:

\[
x^{2/3}= \Bigl(\frac18\Bigr)^{2/3}=2^{-2}= \frac14,\qquad
y^{2/3}= \Bigl(\frac{3\sqrt3}{8}\Bigr)^{2/3}
=3\cdot2^{-2}= \frac34,
\]
so \(x^{2/3}+y^{2/3}=1\). Thus \(C=\bigl(\frac18,\frac{3\sqrt3}{8}\bigr)\).

---

### 3. Compute \(OC^2\)

\[
OC^2 = x^2+y^2
= \left(\frac18\right)^2+\left(\frac{3\sqrt3}{8}\right)^2
= \frac1{64}+\frac{9\cdot3}{64}
= \frac{1+27}{64}
= \frac{28}{64}
= \frac{7}{16}.
\]

Thus \(OC^2=\dfrac{7}{16}\), so \(p=7,\;q=16\) and  

\[
p+q=7+16=23.
\]

\[
\boxed{23}
\]

