The conditions give the following congruences for the digits of \(N=abcd\):

\[
\begin{aligned}
b &\equiv 3a+5 \pmod 7,\\
c &\equiv 2a-1 \pmod 7,\\
d &\equiv 2-a \pmod 7.
\end{aligned}
\]

Requiring each of the numbers obtained by replacing a digit with 1 to be divisible by 7 leads to the single condition

\[
4a+1\equiv0\pmod7 \Longrightarrow a\equiv5\pmod7.
\]

Since \(a\) is a digit, \(a=5\).

Now compute the allowed residues for the other digits:

\[
\begin{aligned}
b &\equiv 3\cdot5+5 =20\equiv6\pmod7 \Longrightarrow b=6,\\
c &\equiv 2\cdot5-1 =9\equiv2\pmod7 \Longrightarrow c\in\{2,9\},\\
d &\equiv 2-5 =-3\equiv4\pmod7 \Longrightarrow d=4.
\end{aligned}
\]

To maximize \(N\) we choose the largest possible \(c\), namely \(c=9\). Thus

\[
N = 5694.
\]

Check:

\[
\begin{aligned}
1694 &=7\cdot242,\\
5194 &=7\cdot742,\\
5614 &=7\cdot802,\\
5691 &=7\cdot813,
\end{aligned}
\]

so the condition holds.

Divide \(N\) by \(1000\):

\[
Q = \left\lfloor\frac{5694}{1000}\right\rfloor = 5,\qquad R = 5694\bmod1000 = 694.
\]

Hence

\[
Q+R = 5+694 = 699.
\]

\[
\boxed{699}
\]

