The number \(N\) must satisfy that replacing any digit by \(1\) yields a multiple of \(7\).

Let \(N = 1000a + 100b + 10c + d\) with digits \(a,b,c,d\) (\(a\neq0\)).  
The four transformed numbers are  

\[
\begin{aligned}
N_a &= 1000 + 100b + 10c + d,\\
N_b &= 1000a + 100 + 10c + d,\\
N_c &= 1000a + 100b + 10 + d,\\
N_d &= 1000a + 100b + 10c + 1.
\end{aligned}
\]

All must be \(0\pmod 7\).

Using modular arithmetic (\(1000\equiv6,\;100\equiv2,\;10\equiv3\pmod7\)), the conditions become  

\[
\begin{cases}
6 + 2b + 3c + d \equiv 0,\\
6a + 2 + 3c + d \equiv 0,\\
6a + 2b + 3 + d \equiv 0,\\
6a + 2b + 3c + 1 \equiv 0 \pmod7.
\end{cases}
\]

Subtracting pairs gives the necessary relations  

\[
b \equiv 3a+5,\qquad c \equiv 2a-1,\qquad d \equiv 2-a \pmod7.
\]

Plugging these residues back into any of the original four congruences shows that the only possible thousands digit is \(a=5\).  
For \(a=5\),

\[
b\equiv6,\quad c\equiv2,\quad d\equiv4\pmod7.
\]

Since digits are between \(0\) and \(9\), we can take  

\[
b=6,\quad c=9\;(=2+7),\quad d=4.
\]

Thus the greatest such four‑digit number is  

\[
N = 5694.
\]

Checking:  

\[
\begin{aligned}
1694 &= 7\cdot242,\\
5194 &= 7\cdot742,\\
5614 &= 7\cdot802,\\
5691 &= 7\cdot813,
\end{aligned}
\]
so the condition holds.

Now divide \(N\) by \(1000\):

\[
Q = \left\lfloor\frac{5694}{1000}\right\rfloor = 5,\qquad 
R = 5694\bmod1000 = 694.
\]

Hence  

\[
Q+R = 5+694 = 699.
\]

\[
\boxed{699}
\]

