The number \(N\) must satisfy that replacing any one of its digits by \(1\) yields a multiple of \(7\).

Let \(N = 1000a + 100b + 10c + d\) with digits \(a,b,c,d\) (\(a\neq0\)).  
The conditions give the congruences  

\[
\begin{aligned}
1000 + 100b + 10c + d &\equiv 0\pmod 7,\\
1000a + 100 + 10c + d &\equiv 0\pmod 7,\\
1000a + 100b + 10 + d &\equiv 0\pmod 7,\\
1000a + 100b + 10c + 1 &\equiv 0\pmod 7.
\end{aligned}
\]

Subtracting these congruences yields  

\[
\begin{aligned}
b &\equiv 3a+5\pmod 7,\\
c &\equiv 2a-1\pmod 7,\\
d &\equiv 2-a\pmod 7.
\end{aligned}
\]

Using the first original congruence and substituting the above expressions gives  

\[
4a+1\equiv0\pmod7\;\Longrightarrow\;a\equiv5\pmod7.
\]

Since \(a\) is a digit, \(a=5\). Then  

\[
b\equiv6\pmod7\Rightarrow b=6,\qquad
c\equiv2\pmod7\Rightarrow c=2\text{ or }9,\qquad
d\equiv4\pmod7\Rightarrow d=4.
\]

Thus the possible numbers are \(5624\) and \(5694\).  
The greatest four‑digit integer satisfying the condition is \(N=5694\).

Now  

\[
Q = \left\lfloor\frac{N}{1000}\right\rfloor = 5,\qquad
R = N\bmod 1000 = 694.
\]

Hence  

\[
Q+R = 5+694 = 699.
\]

\[
\boxed{699}
\]

