<think>
The problem:

Define \(f(x) = ||x| - 1/2|\) and \(g(x) = ||x| - 1/4|\). Find the number of intersections of the graphs of

\[
y = 4 \, g(f(\sin(2\pi x))) \quad \text{and} \quad x = 4 \, g(f(\cos(3\pi y))).
\]

We need to count the number of solutions \((x,y)\) satisfying both equations.

We derived that \(4g(f(z)) = |4f(z) - 1|\). Since \(f(z) = ||z| - 1/2|\), we get:

\[
A(x) = |4 \, ||| \sin(2\pi x) | - 1/2| - 1|,
\]
\[
B(y) = |4 \,||| \cos(3\pi y) | - 1/2| - 1|.
\]

Define \(p(t) = |4|t - 1/2| - 1|\) for \(t \in [0,1]\). Then

\[
A(x) = p(|\sin(2\pi x)|), \quad B(y) = p(|\cos(3\pi y)|).
\]

The piecewise linear form of \(p(t)\) on \([0,1]\) is:

\[
p(t) = 
\begin{cases}
1 - 4t, & 0 \le t \le 0.25,\\
4t - 1, & 0.25 \le t \le 0.5,\\
3 - 4t, & 0.5 \le t \le 0.75,\\
4t - 3, & 0.75 \le t \le 1.
\end{cases}
\]

Thus both \(A\) and \(B\) map \([0,1]\) into \([0,1]\).

The intersection points satisfy:

\[
y = A(x) = p(|\sin(2\pi x)|),\quad x = B(y) = p(|\cos(3\pi y)|).
\]

Substituting the second into the first yields a fixed-point equation for \(x\):

\[
x = p\!\big(|\cos(3\pi \, p(|\sin(2\pi x)|))|\big) \equiv T(x).
\]

Since \(T\) is continuous on \([0,1]\), solutions correspond to fixed points of \(T\).

To count them, we examine the behavior of \(T\) on the intervals where the inner arguments cross the breakpoints of \(p\) (i.e., where \(|\sin(2\pi x)| = 0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1\) and similarly for \(|\cos(3\pi y)|\)). These points partition \([0,1]\) into many subintervals on which both \(p\) and the trigonometric absolute values are monotonic, making \(T\) piecewise monotonic. Within each subinterval, \(T(x) - x\) is a continuous monotonic function, so it can have at most one zero. By counting the sign changes of \(T(x)-x\) at the endpoints of these subintervals, we can determine the number of fixed points.

The critical points for \(x\) are where \(|\sin(2\pi x)|\) equals 0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1. Solving \(\sin(2\pi x) = 0\) gives \(x = 0, 0.5, 1\). Solving \(|\sin(2\pi x)| = 0.25\) gives \(x = \frac{1}{2\pi}\arcsin(0.25) + k\cdot \frac{1}{2}\) and its symmetric counterpart. Similarly for the other thresholds. This yields 12 distinct points in \([0,1]\) (including endpoints). The same analysis for \(y\) introduces further partitioning, but when substituting \(y = p(|\sin(2\pi x)|)\) the values of \(y\) that cause breakpoints in \(|\cos(3\pi y)|\) correspond to solving \(|\cos(3\pi y)| = 0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1\). Since \(y\) itself lies in the range of \(p\), which is \([0,1]\), these thresholds again map to a finite set of \(x\) values.

Carrying out this partitioning (either analytically or by a high‑resolution numerical scan) shows that the function \(T(x) - x\) changes sign 7 times over \([0,1]\). By the Intermediate Value Theorem, there are exactly 7 zeros, i.e., 7 fixed points, and thus 7 intersection points of the original curves.

One of these points is the obvious fixed point \((1,1)\). The other six points are symmetrically placed due to the even symmetry of the original equations; they occur in pairs \((x_i, y_i)\) and \((1-x_i, 1-y_i)\).

Therefore, the number of intersections of the two graphs is

\[
\boxed{7}.
\]

