The family \(\mathcal F\) consists of all unit‑length segments joining a point \(P=(x,0)\) on the \(x\)-axis to a point \(Q=(0,y)\) on the \(y\)-axis, where  

\[
x^2+y^2=1,\qquad x\ge0,\;y\ge0 .
\]

For a given \(\theta\in[0,\pi/2]\) we can write the intercepts as  

\[
x=\cos\theta,\qquad y=\sin\theta .
\]

The line through \(P\) and \(Q\) has equation  

\[
\frac{u}{\cos\theta}+\frac{v}{\sin\theta}=1
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\sin\theta\,u+\cos\theta\,v=\sin\theta\cos\theta .
\tag{1}
\]

---

### 1.  Intersection of a line \(L_\theta\) with \(\overline{AB}\)

Points of \(\overline{AB}\) are parameterised by  

\[
(u,v)=\Bigl(\frac12(1-t),\;\frac{\sqrt3}{2}t\Bigr),\qquad 0\le t\le1 .
\tag{2}
\]

Substituting (2) into (1) gives the value of \(t\) where \(L_\theta\) meets \(\overline{AB}\):

\[
\sin\theta\Bigl(\frac12(1-t)\Bigr)+\cos\theta\Bigl(\frac{\sqrt3}{2}t\Bigr)
      =\sin\theta\cos\theta .
\]

Multiplying by \(2\) and solving for \(t\) (except when \(\theta=\pi/3\), the line coinciding with \(\overline{AB}\)) yields  

\[
t(\theta)=\frac{\sin\theta\,(2\cos\theta-1)}{\sqrt3\cos\theta-\sin\theta}.
\tag{3}
\]

---

### 2.  Behaviour of \(t(\theta)\)

- As \(\theta\to0^+\): \(\sin\theta\sim\theta,\;\cos\theta\sim1\) ⇒ \(t(\theta)\to0\).
- As \(\theta\to\frac\pi2^{-}\): \(\sin\theta\to1,\;\cos\theta\to0\) ⇒ \(t(\theta)\to1\).
- At \(\theta=\frac\pi3\) the denominator and numerator of (3) vanish; taking the limit gives  

\[
\lim_{\theta\to\pi/3}t(\theta)=\frac34 .
\]

The function \(t(\theta)\) is continuous and strictly increasing on each interval \((0,\pi/3)
