Thus we have triangle ABC with base AC. A chain of circles of radius r placed above AC, tangent to AC, and each tangent to neighbors. The first circle's leftmost point touches AB, meaning the distance from its center to AB equals r (since it's tangent). Similarly, the last circle's rightmost point touches BC: distance from its center to BC equals r.

In this case, the centers lie on a line parallel to AC at distance r. The distance between the points where this line intersects AB and BC (i.e., the projection onto AC direction) determines how many circles can fit.

