Thus sin(A/2) = sqrt(39)/13. Then we can find cos(A/2) = sqrt(1 - sin^2) = sqrt(1 - (39/169)) = sqrt((169-39)/169) = sqrt(130/169) = sqrt(130)/13.

Thus tan(A/2) = sin/cos = (sqrt(39)/13) / (sqrt(130)/13) = sqrt(39)/sqrt(130) = sqrt(39/130) = sqrt(3*13 / (10*13))? Actually 130 = 10*13. So 39/130 = 3/10. So tan(A/2) = sqrt(3/10) = sqrt(3)/sqrt(10) = sqrt(30)/10? Actually sqrt(3/10) = sqrt(30)/10? Let's compute: sqrt(3/10) = sqrt(3)/sqrt(10) = sqrt(30)/10? Because sqrt(30)/10 = sqrt(30)/10 = sqrt(30)/10. Multiply numerator and denominator: sqrt(3)/sqrt(10) = sqrt(3*10)/10 = sqrt(30)/10. Yes.

Thus tan(A/2) = sqrt(30)/10.

Now we need AB * AC = c * b.

