We know sin B sin C can be expressed in terms of A? Use identity: sin B sin C = (1/2)[cos(B-C) - cos(B+C)] = (1/2)[cos(B-C) - cos(π - A)] = (1/2)[cos(B-C) + cos A] because cos(π - A) = -cos A, so minus that gives +cos A? Wait: cos(π - A) = -cos A. So -cos(π - A) = -(-cos A) = cos A. Actually identity: sin B sin C = (1/2)[cos(B-C) - cos(B+C)]. B+C = π - A, so cos(B+C) = cos(π - A) = -cos A. So sin B sin C = (1/2)[cos(B-C) - (-cos A)] = (1/2)[cos(B-C) + cos A].

Thus b*c = 4R^2 * (1/2)[cos(B-C) + cos A] = 2R^2 [cos(B-C) + cos A].

