The user provided a problem: Find the least integer b >= 2 for which there are more than ten b-beautiful integers. We derived that the count of b-beautiful integers for base b is 2^{ω(b-1)} - 1, where ω(n) is the number of distinct prime factors of n. We need count > 10 => 2^{ω(b-1)} > 11 => ω(b-1) >= 4. The smallest integer with four distinct prime factors is 210 = 2*3*5*7, so b = 211.

