The number of \(b\)-beautiful integers for a given base \(b\) is determined by the number of distinct prime factors of \(b-1\).

Let \(k=b-1\).  
An integer \(n=s^{2}\) is \(b\)-beautiful iff  

\[
k\mid s(s-1)
\]

with \(2\le s\le k\).  
Since \(\gcd(s,s-1)=1\), for each prime power \(p^{e}\Vert k\) we must have either \(s\equiv0\pmod{p^{e}}\) or \(s\equiv1\pmod{p^{e}}\).  
Thus for each distinct prime divisor of \(k\) there are two choices, giving \(2^{\omega(k)}\) solutions modulo \(k\), where \(\omega(k)\) is the number of distinct prime factors of \(k\).

Among these solutions, \(s=0\) (i.e. \(s=k\)) and \(s=1\) are always present; \(s=1\) is excluded, while \(s=k\) is always valid. Hence the count of \(b\)-beautiful integers is  

\[
\#\,(b\text{-beautiful}) = 2^{\omega(k)}-1.
\]

We need this count to exceed 10:

\[
2^{\omega(k)}-1>10\quad\Longrightarrow\quad2^{\omega(k)}>11\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\omega(k)\ge4.
\]

Thus \(k=b-1\) must have at least four distinct prime factors.  
The smallest integer with four distinct primes is  

\[
2\cdot3\cdot5\cdot7 = 210.
\]

Therefore the smallest base \(b\) is  

\[
b = k+1 = 210+1 = 211.
\]

So the least integer \(b\ge2\) for which there are more than ten \(b\)-beautiful integers is  

\[
\boxed{211}.
\]

