**Solution**

Let the side–lengths of a rectangular box be \(a,b,c>0\).
The surface–area and volume conditions give  

\[
2(ab+bc+ca)=54\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad ab+bc+ca=27,
\]
\[
abc=23 .
\]

For a given box the smallest sphere that contains it is the circumscribed sphere.
Its radius is half the space–diagonal:

\[
r=\frac{\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}}}{2}
\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad 
r^{2}= \frac{a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}}{4}.
\]

Hence we must find the **largest** possible value of  
\(S=a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\) under the two constraints.
(If the sphere has to contain *each* box, its radius must be at least the
largest diagonal/2 among all admissible boxes.)

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Using Lagrange multipliers

Consider  

\[
F(a,b,c,\lambda,\mu)=a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}
+\lambda\,(ab+bc+ca-27)
+\mu\,(abc-23).
\]

Partial derivatives give

\[
\begin{cases}
2a+\lambda(b+c)+\mu bc=0,\\[2pt]
2b+\lambda(a+c)+\mu ac=0,\\[2pt]
2c+\lambda(a+b)+\mu ab=0.
\end{cases}
\]

Subtracting the first two equations yields  

\[
(a-b)\bigl(2-\lambda-\mu c\bigr)=0,
\]
and similarly  

\[
(b-c)\bigl(2-\lambda-\mu a\bigr)=0,\qquad
(a-c)\bigl(2-\lambda-\mu b\bigr)=0.
\]

Thus at any interior extremum either all three sides are equal
(which is impossible because \(a=b=c\) would give \(abc=27\neq23\)),
or at least two of them are equal.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Set two sides equal

Without loss of generality let \(a=b=x\) and \(c=y\).
The constraints become  

\[
x^{2}+2xy=27,\qquad x^{2}y=23.
\]

From the second equation \(y=\dfrac{23}{x^{2}}\). Substituting into the first:

\[
x^{2}+2x\Bigl(\frac{23}{x^{2}}\Bigr)=27
\;\Longrightarrow\;
x^{3}-27x+46=0.
\]

Factoring gives  

\[
(x-2)\bigl(x^{2}+2x-23\bigr)=0,
\]
so the positive solutions are  

\[
x=2,\qquad x=-1+2\sqrt6.
\]

Corresponding \(y\) values are  

\[
y=\frac{23}{4}=5.75\quad\text{when }x=2,
\]
\[
y=\frac{25+4\sqrt6}{23}\quad\text{when }x=-1+2\sqrt6.
\]

Thus the admissible boxes (up to permutation of sides) are  

\[
(2,2,\tfrac{23}{4})\quad\text{and}\quad
\bigl(-1+2\sqrt6,\,-1+2\sqrt6,\,
\tfrac{25+4\sqrt6}{23}\bigr).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Which gives the larger \(S\)?

Compute \(S=a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\) for each case.

*For \((2,2,\tfrac{23}{4})\):*  

\[
S_1=2\cdot2^{2}+\Bigl(\frac{23}{4}\Bigr)^{2}
=8+\frac{529}{16}
=\frac{657}{16}=41.0625.
\]

*For the second box:*  

\[
\begin{aligned}
x^{2}&=25-4\sqrt6,\\
y^{2}&=\frac{(25+4\sqrt6)^{2}}{23^{2}}
      =\frac{721+200\sqrt6}{529}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Hence  

\[
S_2=2x^{2}+y^{2}
=50-8\sqrt6+\frac{721+200\sqrt6}{529}
=\frac{27171-4032\sqrt6}{529}\approx32.69.
\]

Clearly \(S_1>S_2\).  

Since any interior extremum must have at least two equal sides,
and the boundary of the feasible region (where two sides become equal)
has been examined, the maximum of \(S\) is attained at
\((2,2,\tfrac{23}{4})\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Radius of the smallest sphere that contains every box

The sphere that must contain **each** box must have radius at least half the
largest space diagonal, i.e.

\[
r_{\max}= \frac{\sqrt{S_{\max}}}{2}
        =\frac{\sqrt{\tfrac{657}{16}}}{2}
        =\frac{\sqrt{657}}{8}.
\]

Thus  

\[
r^{2}= \frac{657}{64}.
\]

The fraction \(\dfrac{657}{64}\) is already in lowest terms
(\(657=3^{2}\cdot73\), \(64=2^{6}\), no common factor).

Hence  

\[
p=657,\qquad q=64,\qquad p+q=657+64=721.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{721}
\]

