**Solution Explanation**

Let  

\[
\omega = e^{2\pi i/13}
\]

be a primitive 13‑th root of unity.  
We need the remainder of  

\[
P=\prod_{k=0}^{12}\bigl(2-2\omega^{k}+\omega^{2k}\bigr)
\]

when divided by \(1000\).

---

### 1.  Simplify the factor

\[
2-2\omega^{k}+\omega^{2k}= \omega^{2k}-2\omega^{k}+2
= (\omega^{k}-1)^{2}+1 .
\]

Thus  

\[
P=\prod_{\zeta^{13}=1}\bigl(\zeta^{2}-2\zeta+2\bigr),
\]

where the product runs over all 13‑th roots of unity \(\zeta\).

---

### 2.  Use resultants

For monic polynomials \(f(x)=x^{13}-1\) and \(g(x)=x^{2}-2x+2\),

\[
\operatorname{Res}(f,g)=\prod_{f(\alpha)=0}g(\alpha)=\prod_{\zeta^{13}=1}g(\zeta)=P .
\]

The resultant can also be computed as  

\[
\operatorname{Res}(f,g)=\prod_{g(\beta)=0}f(\beta),
\]

because \(g\) is monic.  
So we only need the two roots of \(g(x)=0\).

---

### 3.  Roots of \(g(x)\)

\[
x^{2}-2x+2=0 \Longrightarrow x=1\pm i .
\]

Let  

\[
\beta_{1}=1+i,\qquad \beta_{2}=1-i .
\]

---

### 4.  Evaluate \(f(\beta)=\beta^{13}-1\)

Write \(1+i=\sqrt2\,e^{i\pi/4}\). Then  

\[
(1+i)^{13}= (\sqrt2)^{13}e^{i13\pi/4}
=2^{13/2}e^{i(3\pi+\pi/4)}
=2^{13/2}(-1)e^{i\pi/4}
=-2^{13/2}\frac{\sqrt2}{2}(1+i)
=-64(1+i).
\]

(The same holds for the conjugate: \((1-i)^{13}=-64(1-i)\).)

Hence  

\[
\beta_{1}^{13}-1 = -64(1+i)-1 = -(65+64i),\\
\beta_{2}^{13}-1 = -64(1-i)-1 = -(65-64i).
\]

---

### 5.  Compute the product

\[
P = (-(65+64i))\; (-(65-64i)) = (65+64i)(65-64i)=65^{2}+64^{2}.
\]

\[
65^{2}=4225,\qquad 64^{2}=4096,\qquad
P=4225+4096=8321.
\]

---

### 6.  Remainder modulo \(1000\)

\[
8321 \equiv 321 \pmod{1000}.
\]

---

\[
\boxed{321}
\]



