The torus \(T\) has major radius \(R=6\) and minor radius \(r=3\).  
Place the sphere \(S\) of radius \(11\) so that its centre lies on the axis of revolution of the torus at \((0,0,-h)\).  

A point on the torus can be described in the \(\rho\!-\!z\) cross‑section by  

\[
(\rho-6)^2+z^2=9 .
\]

The sphere’s equation in the same plane is  

\[
\rho^2+(z+h)^2=121 .
\]

Solving these simultaneously gives a quadratic in \(\rho\):

\[
(4h^2+144)\rho^2+(-48h^2-24A)\rho+(108h^2+A^2)=0,
\]
where \(A=148-h^2\).

For the torus to be tangent to the sphere along a circle, this quadratic must have a double root. Setting the discriminant to zero yields

\[
h^2\bigl(h^2-28\bigr)\bigl(h^2-160\bigr)=0,
\]
so \(h^2=28\) or \(h^2=160\).

The corresponding \(\rho\) (the radius of the tangency circle) is

\[
\rho= \frac{3\,(h^2+148)}{h^2+36}.
\]

- For \(h^2=28\): \(\displaystyle \rho=\frac{3(176)}{64}= \frac{33}{4}=8.25\).  
  This is the outer tangency circle, so \(r_o=\frac{33}{4}\).

- For \(h^2=160\): \(\displaystyle \rho=\frac{3(308)}{196}= \frac{33}{7}\approx4.714\).  
  This is the inner tangency circle, so \(r_i=\frac{33}{7}\).

Thus  

\[
r_i-r_o=\frac{33}{7}-\frac{33}{4}= -\frac{99}{28}.
\]

The magnitude of the difference is \(\frac{99}{28}\), already in lowest terms.  
